WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370
- Time for my first guest.

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.980
She became America's mom
on shows like The Middle

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.790
and Everybody Loves Raymond.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.510
Now, she has a new book
out called Your Second Act.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:09.950
Let's give it up for Patricia Heaton.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:10.953
What's up girl?

00:00:10.953 --> 00:00:13.629
- Hi Kelly, how are you?

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:15.830
- I'm good, I'm good.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:16.940
I think we're all hanging in there

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.370
with all the things happening

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:20.500
in everyone's lives right
now, the pandemic, everything,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.850
and everything's crazy and different,

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.180
and for nine or 10 months,
like since you've been

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.013
on our show.

00:00:26.013 --> 00:00:27.040
So where are you right now?

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.780
- Oh my gosh, well right now I'm in LA.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.563
- Okay.
- When the pandemic hit,

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.257
my husband and I were producing,

00:00:35.257 --> 00:00:37.310
and my husband directing a movie

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:40.810
that we are financing in Oklahoma, City.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.540
And so we had five days left

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.060
and the basketball player, Rudy Gobert,

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.260
who was, he had been making
fun of the virus came down

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.530
with the virus and he
was staying at our hotel.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.050
He was staying in our hotel.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.470
So they had to evacuate the hotel

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.210
and then we had to shut
down the production

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.750
with five days left.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:02.782
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:02.782 --> 00:01:03.730
- Yeah.
- What are the chances?

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.690
Five days left.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.170
That's horrible.
- Yes, yes.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.830
- Oh my god, well so
what have you been doing?

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.400
I hear you've been baking a lot.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.310
- Well, yeah.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.400
I mean, you know, we came home, two

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.210
of my sons were working on the movie

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.940
so they came home with me,
and then I have another son

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.120
that was in school here in LA,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.440
so now I have, I went form empty nest back

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.670
to having three of my four sons at home.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:26.760
- That's kind of cool.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.760
- It's kind of cool, yeah,
and, you know, I had a cookbook

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.820
out a while ago and that's come in handy

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.360
and I wish you were here
because, you know, we've got

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.150
some apple pie muffins here

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.370
that are really good.
- Wait, no.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.580
I saw apple pie muffin.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:43.890
I have apple pie muffins.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.690
- You have apple pie muffins?
- I walked into my house today

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:46.947
and I saw a basket of muffins, I was like

00:01:46.947 --> 00:01:48.075
"Is this for the show?"

00:01:48.075 --> 00:01:49.010
- Yes, because they

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.966
go really nice with coffee.
- These were sent to me.

00:01:50.966 --> 00:01:52.750
- Oh my god, they're--

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.791
- My show knows that I love sunflowers,

00:01:54.791 --> 00:01:55.837
- Yes.
- and I see them here

00:01:55.837 --> 00:01:58.110
so I'm trying one.
- And there are big chunks

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.620
of apple in them, and they're very--

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:00.680
- [Kelly] I love apples.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.170
- And they're very easy.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:03.290
Wow.
- Okay, I'm gonna try it.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:04.950
- Okay.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:05.783
It's good, right?

00:02:05.783 --> 00:02:07.570
- Oh my gosh, I love the chink of apple.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.998
- Right, nice big chunks of apple.

00:02:09.998 --> 00:02:10.831
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:10.831 --> 00:02:12.223
So I've been doing a lot of that.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:13.983
- I'm not kidding.

00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:16.410
This is not for the show,
this is really good.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.420
- Let's just east for the rest of this.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.380
- Oh my gosh.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.930
Oh my gosh.
- Yeah.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.060
- So did you all celebrate
anything like during lockdown?

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.900
We had birthdays.
- Well,

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.250
you know, here's the thing.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.810
In July, it was two years
since I've had a drink

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.980
because, two years.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.100
- Yeah.
- Because, as we said,

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.540
I don't think I'm gonna have grand kids

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.050
for many, many years and when
they come, I'll probably be

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.370
about 75, and so I need my brain.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.860
Like I don't wanna be
that drooling grandmother

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.910
in the corner, you know,
that you have to go kiss

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:54.810
and then run away from.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.450
I wanna be active, alive, you know,

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:01.170
so I haven't been drinking
to preserve my brain, right?

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.900
- Yeah.
- However, it doesn't seem

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:03.800
to have helped that much

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.580
because the other night, I get a text

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.490
from my youngest son

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:14.770
and he says "Well, happy
birthday to me, I guess."

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:16.950
And I realized, Kelly--

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.716
- My mother did this once

00:03:18.716 --> 00:03:20.260
in junior high--
- Kelly, not only

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.063
was it his birthday, it
was his 21st birthday.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:25.083
- Oh no.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.820
- I'm at the top of the
stairs, this was like

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.200
before the pandemic so
he was still at school,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.007
I yelled downstairs, "Dave,
we forgot Dan's birthday."

00:03:36.627 --> 00:03:38.087
Dave's like "Oh my god."

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.790
And it's 21st birthday,
so I felt so so bad

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.280
that I started texting
every celebrity friend

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:51.110
that I have and I gave
them Dan's phone number

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:56.207
and I said "Please, will
you text my son Dan,

00:03:56.207 --> 00:03:58.277
"it's his 21st birthday, I forgot.

00:03:58.277 --> 00:04:00.157
"Please just text him,
wish him happy birthday.

00:04:00.157 --> 00:04:02.490
"Tell him I'm a terrible parent."

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.830
And Dan was very impressed, first of all,

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.350
that his roommates in
college were very impressed

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.400
that Ashley Tisdale was texting him.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.650
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh, she's so nice.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.740
- She's so nice and she was
having a conversation with him.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.830
She told me later her husband
said "Who are you texting?"

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:20.642
And she said "A bunch of college boys."

00:04:20.642 --> 00:04:21.780
(laughs loudly)

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.720
But the best one was Ray
Romano sent me a video

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.860
or sent Dan a video and
then he sent it to me

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.160
and it's really funny what he said.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.900
I think you have it.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.763
- Hey Dan, I know it's a little noisy.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.000
I'd understand it's your
21st birthday according

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.163
to my calendar.

00:04:42.118 --> 00:04:45.080
I guess your parents don't
have the same calendar

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.400
that I do, but I keep track of things,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.630
so happy 21st birthday.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.090
- So wait, speaking of Ray
though, the last time you were

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.630
on here, you were talking about

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.550
that you're gonna maybe
play golf with Ray, right?

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:57.570
Did that ever happen?

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:58.570
- It's not happened.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:02.150
In fact, just last week is
the first time I've golfed

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:04.470
in months because of the pandemic, so.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.303
- Yeah.
- I know.

00:05:05.303 --> 00:05:06.560
I think he was just at Pebble Beach

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.010
but I haven't been able to golf with him.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:10.020
- I want his life.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.780
Okay, just hanging out at
Pebble Beach, I'm like.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.050
So Patricia, besides baking,
have you been working

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.170
on anything else at home?

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.520
- My Spanish, I started taking Spanish

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.360
through an app, through Duolingo.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.220
- Me too.
- Are you really?

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.390
- Yes, but unless you
like immerse yourself,

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:28.460
so here's the thing, I
can read it really well

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.240
and I can speak it, if
I'm like, you know, that

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.230
but when it comes to
communication, it's so fast.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:35.545
I can't, I lose it.

00:05:35.545 --> 00:05:38.110
I don't know the
difference between one word

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:39.530
or if it's like two different words

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:40.890
because they say it so fast.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.050
- Well the woman's voice
on it when you have

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:44.340
to repeat what they say or you have

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.770
to type what they said, she's tricky

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.610
because she like mushes words together,

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.330
I'm like "Slow down, you know."

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:54.409
- I know and if I'm not looking at it,

00:05:54.409 --> 00:05:57.020
that's the only thing is I'm really good

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.240
at looking at it, my memory
and vocabulary is all right,

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.180
but it's, and I'm still like early on

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.720
because I just have a lot going on,

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.340
but I just mean like I can't,
the communication part,

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.330
I'm like "Whoa, that is so hard."

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:11.420
And I love the owl shames
you like he'll email you,

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:14.760
like he will email you and be
like "We missed you," tear,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.420
and I'm like "Oh my god, I'm
getting shamed by an owl."

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.890
- Or, you know, you make a mistake

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.180
and he floats onto your screen

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.342
and goes "Hang in there."

00:06:23.342 --> 00:06:24.570
- Yeah, "Hang in there, loser."

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.597
Like it's like "Thank you."

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.100
Okay, well you've got a new book out

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.360
and it's called Your Second Act,

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.760
so obviously the title in front
is what it's probably about

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.390
but go ahead and tell us.
- Yes.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:38.310
Well, interestingly
enough, it was inspired

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.250
by my now canceled TV
show Carol's Second Act.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.370
(laughs loudly)

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:43.203
- I love you.

00:06:44.364 --> 00:06:45.636
(laughs loudly)

00:06:45.636 --> 00:06:47.290
- I'm like what?

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:48.123
Wait what?

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.270
But actually, it kind of is appropriate

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.250
because now I'm having to
like do that next thing

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.410
- Yeah.
- but, you know, fact is

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:00.090
a lot of people, during
this pandemic, they

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:02.980
either lost their job through no faults

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.620
of their own because of the pandemic,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:08.810
or they've been working at
something for a long time

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.220
and they've had these
months to just reflect

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:13.340
on their lives and look, we've all seen

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.490
how sort of precious life is

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:19.250
and how, you know, we can't
take anything for granted,

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:24.170
and so we know that we
have to really use our time

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:26.440
on this planet wisely

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:27.440
- Yeah.
- and to be doing

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:28.310
what we really wanna do.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.520
So I think a lot of people
have had time to reflect,

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.840
and think maybe when we all
get back out there again, I

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:34.610
might wanna do something different.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.740
And so this book is just really, it's

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:39.070
like wonderful, wonderful stories

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.700
of personal friends of mine,

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:42.880
and then just some people we researched

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:46.480
who have changed their life usually

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.690
because of some unforeseen circumstance

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:51.680
that came into their life,
and it's really inspiring,

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.350
and it's little changes like
an industrial carpet salesman

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:59.390
who became an actor, or it's
huge changes like an NFL player

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.090
who became an opera singer.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.310
There's lots of different stories

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:07.120
and there's pages in here
where you can take notes

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.930
and start thinking about how
their stories inspire you.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:13.800
Maybe it's sparking you to
start trying something new.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.980
So I think it's a good time for this book

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.620
because I think people
need some inspiration.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.210
- So do you think that the pandemic,

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.940
you think your worldview,
I feel like everybody,

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:25.090
like you're saying, we're all kind

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.690
of having, I don't know,
a different perspective.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:28.597
You think that's

00:08:28.597 --> 00:08:29.690
happened with you?
- Totally.

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:31.790
Right now, we're starting to think

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.240
about really making some
big changes in our life

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.270
and just figuring out what we wanna do.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.550
We loved producing this movie
and we're really excited

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:42.450
about hopefully finishing it
before the end of the year.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:43.930
- Yeah.
- But that's the direction,

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:45.560
and that's a new direction for me to go

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:49.900
and that's me behind the camera,
you know, assist my husband

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.040
and I loved it.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:53.830
I loved being behind the camera.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:56.730
Oh my gosh, I just thought
watching these actors

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.880
who have to really gee it up every time

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.797
and I mean, I kept thinking

00:09:01.797 --> 00:09:03.540
"Why did I think acting was fun?"

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:04.940
Because I'm watching these actors

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.580
and I'm so relieved I'm
not doing it right now,

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:08.780
but I mean, of course, I love acting

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:10.530
and you know, it'll be
fun to do another show

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:13.840
but I'm really digging
this producing thing a lot.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.390
- Yeah.
- So, you know,

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:16.703
we'll see what happens.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:22.151
You have to look at it as
exciting challenge as opposed to

00:09:22.151 --> 00:09:24.920
- Yeah.
- approaching these new things

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.410
with fear, you have to
start like open yourself up

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:32.680
and allow it to come in and embrace it

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.450
and see where it takes you.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:36.150
See where it takes you.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.210
- Honestly, like in my, I don't care

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:39.260
if we air this or not but,

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:41.400
and I think my life specifically right now

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.100
like there's just a lot going on

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:44.370
- Yeah.
- and it's one of those things

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:45.670
where you can get down about it

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:46.503
but I also have this conversation

00:09:46.503 --> 00:09:47.336
- Yeah.
- with myself

00:09:47.336 --> 00:09:49.520
and I'm like, you know
what, this is opportunity

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.760
and I know it's challenging,
and I know it's not exactly

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.900
what anyone planned or whatever,

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:55.790
but it's one of those
things I like, the messaging

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:58.070
of your book too, and this whole pandemic

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:02.210
in perspective, is
literally that it's okay.

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:04.160
Like opportunity presents
itself, you've no idea

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.480
what that will be and you have no idea

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.520
what beauty might come from
the, you know, darkness

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.460
or the hard times,

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:11.293
- Yeah, it's right.
- and I think that

00:10:11.293 --> 00:10:13.330
that's really cool about,
I love the whole vibe.

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:14.163
I love that you named it Second Act

00:10:14.163 --> 00:10:16.750
and you said it's all
from your canceled show,

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.470
but that makes you one

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.320
of my favorite people
ever, just your humor,

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.390
but I think it's a really good message

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:24.223
and I think you're right.

00:10:24.223 --> 00:10:25.790
I think it's more important now than ever

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:26.800
for people to have that.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:28.560
So thank you so much for
coming on my show again.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.580
- Kelly, thank you for having me.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.360
